Rimegalop
Ingredients: SYMPHONIC & GOTHIC METAL + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Accordion
Creative Catalyst: Channels the hollow slap-back reverberation and wet, bell-like pings of sub-glacial ice chambers calving under the summer sun.

Rimegalop is a thunderous, celebratory clash of operatic gothic metal and frantic folk dance. It welds down-tuned seven-string guitar chugs and blast beats to lightning-fast chromatic accordion runs, church choir arrangements, and triumphant brass fanfares, capturing the ecstatic, reckless revelry of a feast held inside a thawing ice cavern.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 155–190 BPM. Rapid-fire 2/4 and 6/8 beer-hall gallops and polka shuffles driven forward by furious double-bass drumming and crisp, staccato accordion pumping.
- Harmonic Palette: Brooding gothic minor-key church modes (principally D minor and C minor) that routinely pivot via sudden Picardy thirds into radiant, euphoric major cadences, Bavarian folk licks, and soaring Mixolydian choir choruses.
- Production Signatures: True meltwater impulse-response reverb captured inside sub-glacial vaults; close-mic'd, heavily saturated accordion bellows overdriven through tube preamps to match high-gain guitars; pristine, crystalline percussion (glockenspiel, bowed crotales, celesta) layered onto drum transients to simulate brittle ice cracking.
- Dynamics: Abrupt tectonic shifts; songs lurch instantly from intimate, dripping harmonium-and-bellows waltzes into colossal, full-throttle symphonic explosions with operatic vocal polyphony.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre ignited during the record heatwave of 2021 in the sub-glacial drainage tunnels beneath the Jostedalsbreen plateau in Western Norway. A loose collective of Bergen conservatory dropouts, gothic metal session players, and Nordic folk-dance accordionists hauled diesel generators and tube amps down into the cerulean ice tunnels to play for crowds of hikers, metalheads, and glaciologists. The scene, colloquially dubbed the Tine-Gilde (The Thaw Guild), developed its own distinct subcultural flair: 19th-century Victorian mourning attire paired with steel crampons, fluorescent climbing ropes, and floral folk sashes drenched in meltwater silt. Their signature ritual involves dancing furious circle-pits under raining condensation while chugging hot spiced lingonberry cider, treating the literal breakdown of ancient ice not with apocalyptic despair, but with defiant, grinning merriment.

Typical Instruments: Chromatic button accordion, bayan, seven-string electric guitars (drop-A tuning), five-string electric bass, double-kick drum kit, pipe organ/church harmonium, orchestral brass sections, full operatic choir (soprano and baritone duets), and hydrophone field recordings of melting pack ice.

For Fans Of: Finntroll, Nightwish, Korpiklaani, Therion

